bstack is a distillation, in its own words, of ideas from pstack
(https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/main/pstack), which carries this notice:

    MIT License
    Copyright (c) 2026 Lauren Tan

Ideas taken from pstack: routing work to playbooks with evidence gates; proof over
claims ("inconclusive is not a pass"); independent verification by an agent that did
not write the change, bound to the exact patch that merges; subtraction and minimal
diffs; and turning repeated mistakes into checks at the most durable level. The
principles adapted in skills/mode/principles.md follow pstack's principle set
(fix-root-causes, test-behavior-not-implementation, prove-it-works,
encode-lessons-in-structure, boundary-discipline, subtract-before-you-add,
minimize-reader-load, type-system-discipline, never-block-on-the-human).

The planning layer (skills/plan, skills/triage, the ticket playbook) is a distillation,
in its own words, of ideas from mattpocock/skills (https://github.com/mattpocock/skills),
which carries this notice:

    MIT License
    Copyright (c) 2026 Matt Pocock

Ideas taken from mattpocock/skills: grilling in rounds with a recommended answer per
question; a glossary and sparing ADRs as the project's shared language; a map of decision
tickets of four types worked one per session through a frontier of unblocked tickets
(wayfinder); specs and vertical-slice tickets as temporary issues with native sub-issues
and blocking (to-spec, to-tickets); and triage states with agent-ready acceptance criteria.

The guard, risk-tier, verdict hook, tracker script and audit skill are original to bstack.
